<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Modon and Coron</h1><p class="st-meta">1207 CE – 1500 CE · capital Modon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Twin Venetian fortress ports at the south-western tip of the Peloponnese, known as the eyes of the Republic, which guarded Venice's route to the Levant for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> claimed the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in the partition of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after 1204. Venetian forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> (Methoni) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> (Koroni) around 1207, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sapienza">Treaty of Sapienza</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea</a> confirmed them in 1209.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The two ports were governed by Venetian castellans and officials appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and served as stopovers for merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and pilgrim ships bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Their surroundings produced wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and food for the fleets, and they gathered news about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, hence their nickname. Greek, Jewish and Albanian communities lived under Venetian rule.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1500, during the Ottoman-Venetian war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Sultan Bayezid II</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> after a long siege, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> surrendered soon after. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> briefly regained them in 1686-1715 as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/venetian-morea">Venetian Morea</a>. The fortress of Methoni still stands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1207 CE – 1500 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.72%;width:5.83%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1207 CE – 1500 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Modon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Venetian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Methoni and Koroni, Eyes of the Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1354&amp;focus=venetian-modon-and-coron" aria-label="Venetian Modon and Coron on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5233 777 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Venetian Modon and Coron lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5293 831h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5553 1033h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5553 1033h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=1354&amp;focus=venetian-modon-and-coron">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

